<article class="env-lemma" id="047U">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/047U">Lemma <span data-tag="047U">39.7.15</span>.</a> Let $G$ be a group scheme over a field. There exists an open and closed subscheme $G' \subset G$ which is a countable union of affines. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $e \in U(k)$ be a quasi-compact open neighbourhood of the identity element. By replacing $U$ by $U \cap i(U)$ we may assume that $U$ is invariant under the inverse map. As $G$ is separated this is still a quasi-compact set. Set 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  G' = \bigcup \nolimits _{n \geq 1} m_ n(U \times _ k \ldots \times _ k U)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       where $m_ n : G \times _ k \ldots \times _ k G \to G$ is the $n$-slot multiplication map $(g_1, \ldots , g_ n) \mapsto m(m(\ldots (m(g_1, g_2), g_3), \ldots ), g_ n)$. Each of these maps are open (see Lemma <a href="/tag/047K" data-tag="047K">39.7.1</a>) hence $G'$ is an open subgroup scheme. By Lemma <a href="/tag/047S" data-tag="047S">39.7.7</a> it is also a closed subgroup scheme. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>